6.  Q3: omega_c was pinned by GALACTIC orbits AND by LUNAR LASER RANGING -- twelve orders apart.
    IS THAT AN OVER-DETERMINATION, OR DID THE GALACTIC LEG HAVE TO BE IMPOSED?
=========================================================================================================
  LOWER edge (galactic, theory-internal): k(0.90) x MAX omega_gal = 3.0000 x 5.9414e-15 = 1.7824e-14 rad/s
  UPPER edge (LLR, data)                : 2.420e-14/yr x g_N(Moon)/a0 = 2.2097e-14 rad/s (canon) / 1.8289e-14 (alt)
  widths: canon x1.2397   alt x1.0261
   [OK] REGRESSION: widths x1.2397 (canon) and x1.0261 (alt) reproduce mi_omegac_edges_closure_2026.py's x1.2406 / x1.0266 from raw SPARC + the published LLR datum

  *** THE ACTUAL QUESTION, answered directly. ***
  The two constraints are ONE-SIDED and point in OPPOSITE directions:
     LLR      ->  omega_c <= upper      (an UPPER bound; no lower bound whatsoever)
     galaxies ->  omega_c >= lower      (a LOWER bound; no upper bound whatsoever)
  A one-sided upper bound CANNOT imply a lower bound.  So the galactic requirement is NOT
  delivered free by LLR -- it is logically independent and had to be IMPOSED.  What LLR alone
  DOES deliver is a CEILING on the retention the gate can achieve at the binding galactic orbit:
     Re G(omega_gal / upper) = 0.9326   <- the BEST retention LLR permits
     the framework needs      >= 0.9000
     margin in retention      = +0.0326   (critical retention S_crit = 0.9326)
   [OK] THE CONSISTENCY TEST PASSES, and it is a real test that could have failed: LLR's ceiling permits at most Re G = 0.9326 at UGC05721's innermost deep-MOND orbit, and the framework needs 0.90.  The margin is 0.0326 in retention

  HOW MUCH of the LLR-allowed range satisfies the galactic leg?  On a log measure over the
  physically bracketed range omega_c in [MAX omega_gal, upper] (below MAX omega_gal the gate is
  shut on the rotation curves entirely, so that is the natural floor):
     surviving log-fraction = ln(upper/lower)/ln(upper/omega_gal) = 0.1636 = 16.4%
   [OK] only 16.4% of the LLR-permitted log-range clears the galactic requirement.  So the galactic leg REMOVES 83.6% of what LLR allows -- it is doing real work, which is the precise sense in which the window is NOT an automatic consequence of LLR

  *** THE TRANSFERABLE OVER-DETERMINATION THAT IS ACTUALLY THERE. ***
  Requiring the window to be NON-EMPTY (upper >= lower) eliminates omega_c and leaves a bound
  on a0 with NO RAR fit, NO M/L inference and NO slope estimator anywhere in it:
     a0 <= (ceiling/yr) g_N(Moon) / (k(S) MAX omega_gal) = 1.1606e-10 m/s2
     canonical 9.3614e-11 clears it by x1.2397;  ALT 1.1311e-10 clears it by x1.0261
   [OK] SPARC ORBITAL FREQUENCIES + LLR ALONE give a0 <= 1.1606e-10 -- a genuinely SECOND, methodologically disjoint line on a0.  It clears canon by 24.0% and the ALT footing by only 2.6%.  That IS scale-bridging over-determination, and it is a bound, not a value

  IS omega_c NATURAL AT THE WINDOW?  The framework's own candidate scales:
     a0/c  (Route B at v = c)           3.1226e-19 rad/s   = x1.752e-05 the window's lower edge
     a0/v_rel, v = 220 km/s (MW)        4.2552e-16 rad/s   = x2.387e-02 the window's lower edge
     a0/v_rel, v = 30 km/s (dwarf)      3.1205e-15 rad/s   = x1.751e-01 the window's lower edge
     H_Lambda = sqrt(Lambda/3) c        1.8076e-18 rad/s   = x1.014e-04 the window's lower edge
     1/t_age                            2.2967e-18 rad/s   = x1.289e-04 the window's lower edge
   [OK] AGAINST INTEREST: not one of the framework's own natural frequency scales lands in the window -- the closest misses by a factor 5.7, and Route B's a0/c is 5.7e+04x BELOW the lower edge.  omega_c is TUNED to sit there, and a tuned constant bracketed by two bounds is a fit with a consistency check, not a derivation

  AND THE STRUCTURAL FORK THAT SITS UNDER BOTH EDGES, stated because it is load-bearing:
  mi_dcac_split_settled_2026.py proved a MUTUAL-EXCLUSIVITY theorem for the closure that feeds
  the memory kernel.  Under the FIRST-MOMENT closure -- the one that reproduces the RAR at
  0.108 dex -- the kernel's argument is |a|^2/a0^2 >= 0, K is real, and the DISSIPATIVE channel
  is IDENTICALLY ZERO, not merely small.  The LLR drift d ln a/dt = a0 omega_c/g_N IS that
  channel.  Under the LITERAL spectral closure the drift exists but the RAR fails outright.
  So the upper edge and the rotation curves are computed under closures the corpus has itself
  proved mutually exclusive.  Two readings, two consequences for omega_c's STATUS:
     first-moment closure  -> drift == 0 -> LLR is BLIND -> omega_c UNCONSTRAINED ABOVE,
                              and the gate's Re G is likewise not sampled: omega_c becomes
                              an UNOBSERVABLE label, which REMOVES it from the dof count
                              without any over-determination having occurred.
     literal spectral      -> omega_c observable, window as computed, but no rotation curves.
  Neither reading delivers 'omega_c derived'.  Reported as the spread, not resolved here.

